WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.315
It is eight a.m.

00:00:01.315 --> 00:00:06.248
A school needs a Year Six maths teacher in Grimsby for nine.

00:00:06.248 --> 00:00:13.236
Edwina brings portal, WhatsApp and consultant-logged phone requests into one booking.

00:00:13.236 --> 00:00:18.909
This demonstration uses illustrative data and simulated integrations.

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:25.515
Sarah sees the requirement, deadline and next step in one workspace.

00:00:25.515 --> 00:00:34.739
Instead of searching thousands of profiles manually, she starts with two eligible educators, ordered by suitability.

00:00:35.939 --> 00:00:46.750
The filters explain the result: relevant experience, travel preferences, current checks, calendar availability and arrival time.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.115
Every requirement must pass.

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:56.717
Only then are eligible candidates ranked using experience, previous MAT work and feedback.

00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:06.480
Ben sees the request on his Today page, alongside availability, timesheets, payroll setup and learning.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.888
The urgent assignment is the main action.

00:01:11.088 --> 00:01:14.846
Ben confirms that he can leave within five minutes.

00:01:14.846 --> 00:01:20.077
His reply can come through the app or the proposed WhatsApp connection.

00:01:20.077 --> 00:01:25.014
An available calendar is not assumed to mean he is ready to travel.

00:01:26.214 --> 00:01:31.890
Sarah can now see Ben’s readiness, response time and expected arrival.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:39.350
His experience and previous MAT work remain visible beside one clear action: send the offer.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:46.333
Ben reviews the school, pay, working hours and arrival instructions.

00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:49.820
He acknowledges the briefing and accepts.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.711
Everyone follows the same booking.

00:01:53.911 --> 00:02:02.359
The proposed llama dot I D connection supplies screening evidence, with Edwin retaining the clearance decision.

00:02:02.359 --> 00:02:09.132
Once Ben accepts and checks pass, the proposed Bullhorn connection records the placement.

00:02:09.132 --> 00:02:12.480
Sarah does not need to re-enter the booking.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.794
The school sees Ben confirmed, his expected arrival and a link to the booking.

00:02:19.794 --> 00:02:25.281
Helen can focus on welcoming the educator rather than chasing updates.

00:02:26.481 --> 00:02:30.469
Helen sends reception instructions directly to Ben.

00:02:30.469 --> 00:02:37.507
She can also contact Sarah at Edwin in a separate conversation attached to the assignment.

00:02:38.707 --> 00:02:43.053
Ben receives the school’s instructions in the same assignment.

00:02:43.053 --> 00:02:50.203
School-to-candidate communication and the named Edwin contact remain available throughout the booking.

00:02:51.403 --> 00:03:00.403
Candidates can also browse recommended jobs, compare dates, pay and journey information, and register interest.

00:03:00.403 --> 00:03:06.078
Their schedule, pay records and learning remain part of the wider app.

00:03:07.278 --> 00:03:16.651
Rachel’s trust dashboard brings together spend against budget, fill rates, time to fill, open cover and school comparisons.

00:03:16.651 --> 00:03:23.738
She can move from an organisation-wide view into the schools and records that need attention.

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:28.760
Reporting goes beyond the headline dashboard.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:39.971
Choose monthly spend, last academic year, role, education type, booking type, reason for cover, outstanding timesheets or assurance.

00:03:39.971 --> 00:03:43.538
School and date filters define the report.

00:03:44.738 --> 00:03:53.291
The charts make role mix, service-provider spend, reasons for cover and school absence comparisons easier to explore.

00:03:53.291 --> 00:04:00.528
Each view can lead into its supporting report, rather than leaving the user with a headline number.

00:04:01.728 --> 00:04:06.259
Rachel selects spend by job role for the chosen period.

00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:14.249
She can create a PDF to circulate or export the filtered figures into Excel for further analysis.

00:04:15.449 --> 00:04:27.485
This is the report produced by the prototype’s PDF generator: the selected dates, school scope, role totals and definitions travel with the data.

00:04:27.485 --> 00:04:32.134
The Excel export preserves numeric amounts for analysis.

00:04:33.334 --> 00:04:41.701
The term outlook shows when cover demand may rise, with actual observations separated from the forecast scenario.

00:04:41.701 --> 00:04:47.329
Rachel can inspect the weekly values and planning assumptions before acting.

00:04:48.529 --> 00:04:56.342
The monthly cost view combines worked spend, confirmed future cover and an allowance for further demand.

00:04:56.342 --> 00:05:03.779
It shows a central scenario and range, helping the trust discuss likely budget pressure with Edwin.

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:10.314
Rachel can test higher demand and review the requirements by school.

00:05:10.314 --> 00:05:14.158
Confirmed cover is separated from further demand.

00:05:14.158 --> 00:05:19.964
She can review a requirement with Sarah before arranging additional cover.

00:05:19.964 --> 00:05:24.592
Forecasts here are illustrative, not validated predictions.

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:30.094
Now consider a separate request, E D M two oh one.

00:05:30.094 --> 00:05:37.492
BrightPath sees its own opportunities, capacity declarations, assignments and finance.

00:05:37.492 --> 00:05:42.138
Ben’s earlier confirmed booking is a different record.

00:05:43.338 --> 00:05:50.605
Olivia proposes Eva, confirms availability and provides the charge to Edwin and the evidence pack.

00:05:50.605 --> 00:05:56.537
Internal purchasing information stays separate from the school’s agreed booking.

00:05:57.737 --> 00:06:02.969
BrightPath can track its submitted educator and evidence status.

00:06:02.969 --> 00:06:06.730
Edwin owns the review and school relationship.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:11.308
The school continues to use its usual booking interface.

00:06:12.508 --> 00:06:17.406
A separate rollout illustration shows exception-led handling.

00:06:17.406 --> 00:06:22.785
After Ben confirms readiness, Edwina sends the offer automatically.

00:06:22.785 --> 00:06:30.172
Start with consultant approvals, then automate agreed routine steps as evidence supports it.

00:06:30.172 --> 00:06:34.266
Human oversight and the same booking checks remain.

00:06:35.466 --> 00:06:46.193
The result is a connected service: suitable educators, timely responses, confirmed cover, visible ownership and useful trust insight.

00:06:46.193 --> 00:06:53.775
Consultants can spend more time on relationships while Edwina handles repeatable coordination.
